INTERPRETATION

LU-2D establishes a genuine
authority-amplitude effect on developmental trajectories.

More learned U authority causes:

more realized U transition differences;

and more persistent divergence
from the exact teacher state trajectory.

However that trajectory divergence
does not translate monotonically
into worse Task-1 function.

The organism is functionally robust
to substantial developmental path differences.

This is an important distinction.

The relevant control problem is not simply:

more divergence = worse function.

Instead:

learned authority changes developmental history strongly,
while the task-level organism often absorbs those changes.

The 25-percent authority arm is especially notable:

it changes real U decisions;

its median state trajectory differs
for roughly three quarters of epochs;

yet all ten worlds remain inside
the exact LU-2A/LU-2B functional envelope.

PROMOTION BOUNDARY

Do not promote U_A25 from this experiment alone.

LU-2D used the same worlds
to characterize the authority dose response
and observe low-dose functional preservation.

Per preregistration,
a completely fresh independent confirmation
is required before treating U_A25
as a stable bounded-authority candidate.

Do not select a new alpha.

Do not tune 0.25.

Do not relax any functional bar.

NEXT JUSTIFIED STEP

Run an independent confirmation of:

H:
exact V0.2 teacher.

U:
alpha = 0.25 fixed.

C/S:
exact V0.2 teacher.

Use a new preregistered namespace
and completely fresh worlds.

Require:

causal U participation;

duplicate execution identity;

and the exact same LU-2A/LU-2B functional-preservation bars.

If that confirmation passes,
25-percent learned U authority
becomes a candidate bounded-authority mechanism.

It still would not establish:

full learned U authority;

learned C/S authority;

or a fully learned developmental cell.

PLAIN-SPEAK INTERPRETATION

Turning the neural recruiter's influence up
does exactly what we suspected
to the organism's developmental path.

At 25 percent,
the neural recruiter changed 59 real decisions.

At 50 percent,
147.

At full authority,
346.

And the stronger the neural influence,
the more of the organism's history
ended up different from the hand-written version.

But the surprising part is:

different development did not automatically mean worse function.

The 25-percent neural organism
had a different cell-state configuration
for about three quarters of the run,
yet every one of ten worlds
still passed all of the functional bars.

So the organism appears more functionally resilient
than its internal trajectory is stable.

That makes 25-percent learned recruitment
worth confirming independently.

Not promoting yet.

Confirming.

